;; Lesson 4: water from tank to tank (#185). Read by src/HeroicInventions.Sim/Editor/Lessons.cs;
;; the format is described there.
;;
;; The numbers, worked by hand from the sim's own pipe model (FluidNetwork.cs: the flow through a pipe is its
;; conductance k times the difference of the two water heights, q = k (h_upper - h_lower), as in racket/machines/
;; cistern-and-trough.rkt; a pipe's default k is 0.001 m³/s for each metre of difference):
;;   the starter tank: 0.05 m² across, 0.3 m high, comes with 0.010 m³ = 10 litres = 0.2 m deep
;;   the upper one stands on a 1 m post (its floor at 1.0 m), the lower one on the ground (its floor at 0),
;;   both pipe connections at the floors; the lower one is poured out first (0 litres)
;;   a = depth in the upper tank, b = depth in the lower one; the 10 litres are shared between them, so a + b = 0.2 m
;;   the difference of the two water heights: D = (1.0 + a) - b = 0.8 + 2a
;;   A da/dt = -k D   ->   da/dt = -(0.001 / 0.05)(0.8 + 2a) = -0.02 (0.8 + 2a)
;;   so  a(t) = -0.4 + 0.6 e^(-0.04 t)   (time constant A / 2k = 25 s)
;;   the upper tank runs dry when e^(-0.04 t) = 0.4/0.6, t = ln(1.5) / 0.04 = 10.14 s
;;   all 10 litres have moved by then: the lower tank holds 10 litres, 0.2 m deep (the lower floor is below the upper one,
;;   so the water never evens out: the upper empties first). The test ends a second after the water stops, at 11.1 s.
;; So the check: the lower tank ends within 0.5 litre of 10 litres (and the upper one has none left).
;; Measured: the editor's Test, 10.0 litres in the lower tank, ending at 11.1 s (see lesson-test.rkt).
;;
;; The experiment (#197), water won't run uphill: after the first Test the lesson stands the lower tank on a 2 m post (its floor at
;; 2.0 m), above the 1.2 m the upper tank's water reaches (1.0 m floor + 0.2 m). A pipe's flow is k (h_upper - h_lower) from the higher
;; surface to the lower, and a tank cannot give what it does not hold: the lower one is empty, so its surface is its floor, 2.0 m, 0.8 m
;; above the water in the upper one, and nothing can flow from it. The upper tank keeps its 10 litres, the lower one has 0:
;; q = 0 for the whole Test (it ends at its 4 s minimum). Traced: no water moved, the Test ended at 4.01 s.
;; The check: the lower tank ends within 0.5 litre of 0 and the upper one within 0.5 litre of 10.

(lesson water
  (title "Lesson 4: water from tank to tank")
  (view 0.2 0.3 3.2 0.4 0.95 0)

  (step
    (text "Water runs downhill, so put a tank up high. First a post: pick Post in the parts list (it is lit up), then click on the green shape in the scene.")
    (highlight post)
    (target (kind post) (at 0 0 0) (within 0.5))
    (check place (kind post) (as post)))

  (step
    (text "Put a water tank on top of the post, on the green shape. Pick Water tank: it comes with {upper.litres} of water in it, that is {upper.level} deep.")
    (highlight tank)
    (target (kind tank) (at 0 1 0) (within 0.25))
    (check place (kind tank) (as upper)))

  (step
    (text "A second tank on the ground, to the right of the post, on the green shape. The lesson pours it out first: it starts empty.")
    (highlight tank)
    (target (kind tank) (at 0.8 0 0) (within 0.25))
    (check place (kind tank) (as lower)
           (then "(set {id} #:water 0)")))

  (step
    (text "Join the two with a pipe. Each tank has a blue dot low on its front, labelled \"water in / out\" when the tank is selected: that is where water goes in and out. Click the dot on the upper tank, then the dot on the lower tank, and a pipe joins them.")
    (check join (kind pipe) (between upper lower)))

  (step
    (text "Press Test it (lit, top left) and watch the water. The pipe lets through 1 litre a second for each metre the water in one tank stands above the other. The top of the upper tank's water is 1.2 m up and the lower tank's is at the floor, so at first the water runs at about 1.2 litres a second, slowing as the upper tank sinks and the lower one fills. The upper tank runs dry after about {empties}, and all {upper.litres} should end up in the lower one.")
    (highlight test)
    (fail "The lower tank ended with less water than the {upper.litres} the upper one had: it should hold all of it. Is the pipe joined to a dot on each tank, and is the lower tank below the upper? Change it and press Test it again.")
    (check level (part lower) (litres 10) (within 0.5)
           (then "(move {lower} (0.8 2 0))")
           (then "(post tower #:at (0.8 0 0) #:size-y 2 #:material oak)")))

  (step
    (text "That is water running downhill. Now try to make it run uphill: the lesson has stood the lower tank up on a tall post, 2 m high, higher than the water in the upper tank (which stands 1.2 m up). The pipe is still joined to both. Work it out before you watch. The pipe only lets water through from the higher surface to the lower one. Now the lower tank's floor, at 2 m, is above all the water in the upper one, and the lower tank holds none, so there is nothing to come down from it. No water should move at all: the upper tank keeps its {upper.litres}, and the lower one stays empty. Press Test it.")
    (highlight test)
    (view 0.2 0.25 5 0.4 2.0 0)
    (fail "Some water moved: the lower tank's water changed by {moved}, but this time none should run uphill. Is the lower tank on the tall post, above the water in the upper one, with the pipe still joined to both? Put it back, or press Start over, and press Test it again.")
    (check level (part lower) (litres 0) (within 0.5))
    (check level (part upper) (litres 10) (within 0.5)))


  (done "No water moved: the upper tank kept its {upper.litres} and the lower one stayed empty. A pipe lets water go from the higher surface to the lower one, never the other way, and the lower tank now stood above the upper one's water. In the first Test the same pipe ran all ten litres downhill; raised, it carries nothing. Water never runs uphill by itself: to lift it you need a pump, or a water wheel, or a lot of people with buckets."))
